<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Taifa of Córdoba</h1><p class="st-meta">1031 CE – 1070 CE · capital Córdoba</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The former caliphal capital governed as a council-led republic by the Jahwarid family after the caliphate was formally abolished in 1031.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1031 the notables of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, exhausted by two decades of civil war, deposed the last Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-iii-of-cordoba">Hisham III</a>, and abolished the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-hazm-ibn-jahwar">Abu al-Hazm ibn Jahwar</a> became head of a government that ruled through a council of leading citizens.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahwarid-dynasty">Jahwarids</a> presented themselves as administrators rather than kings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-hazm-ibn-jahwar">Abu al-Hazm</a> restored order and finances, avoided wars and maintained the city's markets and scholarship, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> had lost much of its population and grandeur. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-zaydun">Ibn Zaydun</a> served the regime before falling out with it.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Abd al-Malik ibn Jahwar's rule ended when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutamid-ibn-abbad">al-Mutamid</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taifa-of-seville">Taifa of Seville</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in 1070. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> held it from 1075 until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> recovered it in 1078. The city later rose again as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-taifa-of-cordoba">Second Taifa of Córdoba</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/taifa-of-cordoba.jpg" alt="Taifa of Córdoba" width="320" height="271"><figcaption>Image: Tyk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_map_Taifa_of_C%C3%B3rdoba.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1031 CE – 1070 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Córdoba</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abu al-Hazm ibn Jahwar, Abu al-Walid Muhammad ibn Jahwar, Abd al-Malik ibn Jahwar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic, Mozarabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jahwarid Córdoba</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1050&amp;focus=taifa-of-cordoba" aria-label="Taifa of Córdoba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4561 815 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taifa of Córdoba lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4881 1071L4881 1071M4881 1071L4850 1088"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4881 1071h0M4850 1088h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4881 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4881 1071h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Córdoba. <a href="/atlas?year=1050&amp;focus=taifa-of-cordoba">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
